s a reflection grating. Theoretical confirmation: **Declercq, N.F., Degrieck, J., Leroy, O. et al. (2004). "A theoretical study of special acoustic effects caused by the staircase of the El Castillo pyramid at the Maya ruins of Chichen-Itza in Mexico." *JASA* 116(6): 3328–3335.** Note Declercq later showed ANY comparable periodic staircase produces a chirp (duck-like echoes at other temples), weakening the intentionality case — the *effect* is solid physics; the *design intent* is unverifiable.
- Related: Lubman & Kiser (2001), ASA abstract on the Great Ballcourt acoustics; the Ballcourt "whispering gallery" claim traces to Morley (1925) and is plausible waveguide physics along 8.2 m parallel walls over ~140–168 m.

### 1.6 Till / Cambridge Soundscapes / Scarre & Lawson
- The 2003 Cambridge conference "Acoustics, Space and Intentionality" produced the field's anchor volume: **Scarre, C. & Lawson, G. (eds) (2006). *Archaeoacoustics.* McDonald Institute Monographs, Cambridge.** Contributors include Watson, Devereux, Reznikoff, Waller, Till. (No "Burgess" author in the canon — likely a confusion; possibly the lead means this Cambridge "soundscapes" program.)
- **Till, R. (2010/2009). "Songs of the Stones: An Investigation into the Acoustic Culture/History of Stonehenge." IASPM Journal 1(2)** — measurements at the Maryhill (Washington) full-size concrete replica: argued the complete circle behaved as an enclosed reverberant space, not an open ring. URL: https://iaspmjournal.net/index.php/IASPM_Journal/article/view/308
- **Till, R., Wyatt, S., Fazenda, B., Sheaffer, J. & Scarre, C. — "Songs of the Caves" project** (Tito Bustillo cave, Asturias; impulse-response measurement of painted caves): https://songsofthecaves.wordpress.com/
- **Till, R. (2019). "Sound archaeology: a study of the acoustics of three World Heritage sites: Spanish prehistoric painted caves, Stonehenge, and Paphos theatre." *Acoustics* (MDPI) 1(3): 661–692.** DOI: 10.3390/acoustics1030039. — calibrated ISO 3382-style room-acoustic metrics (T20/T30, EDT, C50, C80, STI) across sites.

### 1.7 Stonehenge scale model (2020)
**Cox, T.J., Fazenda, B.M. & Greaney, S.E. (2020). "Using scale modelling to assess the prehistoric acoustics of Stonehenge." *Journal of Archaeological Science* 122: 105218.** DOI: 10.1016/j.jas.2020.105218.
- 1:12 physical scale model of the complete ~2200 BC monument in a semi-anechoic chamber (ultrasonic excitation up to 70 kHz ≙ 5.8 kHz full-scale), plus measurements at the Maryhill replica and the current ruin.
- **Exact results: average mid-frequency reverberation time 0.64 ± 0.03 s; speech-band amplification 4.3 ± 0.9 dB** inside the circle vs. outside; bluestone reconfigurations made inaudible differences → authors explicitly conclude sound was "unlikely to be the underlying motivation" for the arrangements.
- Precursor modeling paper: **Fazenda, B.M. & Drumm, I.C. (2013). "Recreating the sound of Stonehenge." *Acta Acustica united with Acustica* 99(1): 110–117.** DOI: 10.3813/AAA.508592.
- URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0305440320301394 ; method write-up: https://acousticengineering.wordpress.com/2020/01/22/how-we-measured-the-acoustic-scale-model-of-stonehenge/

